Kubernetes Troubleshooting Interview Questions — Real-World Scenarios


1. A Kubernetes Pod is down. How would you troubleshoot it?
My first step would be to understand why the pod is down, rather than immediately restarting it.

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

I would check the pod status, events and application logs.
If the container restarted:

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

Then based on the error — CrashLoopBackOff, OOMKilled, ImagePullBackOff, Pending, probe failure, etc. — I would troubleshoot that specific layer.

2. A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. What would you check?
This means the container is starting and then repeatedly crashing.
I would start with:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous
I would check application startup errors, environment variables, ConfigMaps/Secrets, database connectivity, command/entrypoint configuration and health probes.

3. A Pod is stuck in Pending. What could be wrong?
First:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
The Events section usually gives an important clue.
Possible causes include:
Insufficient CPU or memory on worker nodes
Node selector/affinity mismatch
Taints and tolerations
PVC/storage issues
No suitable worker node available
Then I would check:
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>
kubectl get pvc

4. What would you do for ImagePullBackOff?
I would check:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Then verify the image name and tag.
For EKS + ECR, I would also check whether the worker node or workload has the required permissions and whether the private nodes have network connectivity to ECR.

5. A container shows OOMKilled. What does that mean?
It normally means the container exceeded its configured memory limit.
I would check:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl top pod <pod-name>
Then I would check the application's actual memory consumption and its Kubernetes requests/limits.
I wouldn't simply increase the memory immediately — I would also check whether there is a memory leak or abnormal application behavior.

6. The Pod is Running, but the application isn't working. What would you check?
Running doesn't necessarily mean the application is healthy.
I would check:
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl get svc
kubectl get endpoints
I would particularly check readiness probes, application logs, Service selectors and endpoints.
